;; `cli` and `test` are deliberately NOT in :paths.
;;
;; Every path listed at the top level lands on the classpath of anything
;; that resolves this port, so :paths is the library and nothing else. The
;; CLI is an example program and the conformance suite is this port's own
;; test; neither is part of what a consumer takes, and the aliases below add
;; them back for the Makefile.
;;
;; `src` and `plugins` are BOTH the library, and the split between them is
;; the boundary this port exists to keep: `src` is the chain, the four
;; built-in kinds and the plugin bridge, and it requires nothing under
;; `plugins`. The ten kinds that open a socket, sign a request or spawn a
;; process live under `plugins`, one namespace each, and a chain may name
;; one only if the calling project passed the definition in. `make
;; check-core` builds `src` with `plugins` off the classpath entirely, which
;; is what makes the rule a fact rather than a habit.
;;
;; :deps is empty because the library's one dependency is voxgig/plugin,
;; which has no published Clojure artifact and no deps.edn of its own: the
;; Makefile puts its source directory on the classpath, the way the other
;; JVM port passes a path to its compiler. There is no JSON library here
;; either, and no HTTP client - see src/voxgig/sekreto/json.clj and the
;; java.net.http interop in plugins/voxgig/sekreto/plugins/httpjson.clj.
{:paths ["src" "plugins"]
 :deps {}
 :aliases
 {;; The CLI, and the directory its AOT-compiled classes are written to:
  ;; Clojure's compiler requires *compile-path* to be on the classpath.
  :cli {:extra-paths ["cli" "build/classes"]}

  :test {:extra-paths ["test"]}}}
